          "path": "_m/g/b/g/u/porthcurno-wp/footpath-through-the-maize-tow.jpg",
          "filename": "Footpath through the maize towards Treen - geograph.org.uk - 230342.jpg",
          "caption": "Footpath through the maize towards Treen. This footpath through the maize crop links the iron-age promontory castle at Treryn Dinas with the hamlet of Treen. In the usual Cornish way the hamlet is literally named after the nearby cliff castle. In 1478 it was written \"Trethyn\"... \"Tre\" meaning 'farm/settlement' and \"din\" meaning 'fort'. The d/th part has been lost over the years. Confusingly enough there is another hamlet called Treen on the north coast, near the iron age cliff castle on the Gurnard's Head peninsula.",
          "alt": "Footpath through the maize towards Treen. This footpath through the maize crop links the iron-age promontory castle at Treryn Dinas with the hamlet of Treen. In the usual Cornish way the hamlet is literally named after the nearby cliff castle. In 1478 it was written \"Trethyn\"... \"Tre\" meaning 'farm/settlement' and \"din\" meaning 'fort'. The d/th part has been lost over the years. Confusingly enough there is another hamlet called Treen on the north coast, near the iron age cliff castle on the Gurnard's Head peninsula.",
          "width": 480,
          "height": 640,
          "mimeType": "image/jpeg",
          "license": "CC BY-SA 2.0",
